# HTML pages in {{ .Name }}

Pages are templ components rendered by plain `net/http` handlers inside the layout.
`internal/home` is the working example: `views/home.templ` (the component),
`handler.go` (`Register` and the handler), `handler_test.go` (its test).
`web/layout/layout.templ` is the shell (title, CSRF meta tag, flashes, htmx and
Alpine.js); `static/` holds the embedded assets. Recipes: `add-html-page`, `add-form-with-validation`, `add-htmx-partial`,
`call-backend-api`, `translate-server-strings` (`docs/recipes/` in the gox module
directory).

## Add a page

1. Write the component in `internal/<feature>/views/<page>.templ`. It takes typed
   parameters and never reads the request.
2. Run `make generate`. It writes `<page>_templ.go` with the templ version pinned in
   the Makefile; until then the package does not compile (`undefined: views.<Page>`).
   Commit the generated files: CI does not generate them.
3. In the handler set `web.PageFrom(r).Title` first, then
   `_ = web.Render(w, r, views.<Page>(...))`. The layout prints the title before the
   body renders, so a title set later is lost.
4. Mount the route in the feature's `Register(a, deps...)` and call that from main.
5. Test it (below), then `make test lint`.

Pick the writer:

- `web.Render`: the page inside the layout; for an htmx request that is not an
  `hx-boost` navigation it sends the component alone.
- `web.RenderStatus`: the same with a status (422 for a form with errors).
- `web.Partial`: the component alone, always (htmx fragments).
- `gox.Error` (`web.Error` is the same function): failures. Browsers get the error
  page, API clients the JSON envelope, with the same status.

## Forms

`GET` renders the form. `POST` decodes with `web.Form[T]`; on `FieldErrors` it
re-renders with `web.RenderStatus` and 422, else `web.AddFlash` and `web.Redirect`
(303, or `HX-Redirect` for htmx). Code: recipe `add-form-with-validation`.

- Every `<form method="post">` contains `@web.CSRFField(page)`, so the component
  takes `page *web.Page`. A form post without the token is a 403. htmx requests
  send the token from `@web.CSRFMeta(page)` and the script in the layout: keep both.
- `web.FieldErrors` is keyed by the `form` tag name (`errs["email"]`).
  `go doc github.com/guilhermebr/gox/web.Form` lists the tags and the messages.
- JSON requests are never CSRF-checked.

## htmx, Alpine.js and static files

- Run `make assets` once: it vendors the pinned htmx and Alpine.js into
  `static/js`, which the layout loads. Until then `hx-*` and `x-*` attributes do
  nothing.
- Link assets with `page.Asset("css/app.css")`: production URLs carry a content hash.
- `static/static.go` embeds `css` and `js` only: add a new directory (`img`) to its
  `go:embed` line or it is not served.
- An htmx endpoint answers with `web.Partial`; a redirect after an htmx post is
  `web.Redirect`, which htmx follows.

## templ gotchas

- Never put a `for` or `if` block on one line: `for _, x := range xs { <li>{ x }</li> }`
  fails to parse. Open the brace, put the children on their own lines, close it on
  its own line.
- Text that starts with a keyword (`for`, `if`) inside an element is read as a
  statement: write it as `{ "for" }`.
- Pages take what they show as parameters. `web.PageFrom(r).Data` is for the layout only.

## Signed-in users and the API

- `web.RequireSession(handler)` sends anonymous visitors to `/login`.
- `web.WithSessionUser` resolves `page.User` once per request.
- `web.APIFrom(r)` calls this service's JSON API as the signed-in user; it needs
  `web.WithBackend()` and `gox.HTTPClient()`. Recipe `call-backend-api`.

## Test

- Components: `views.Home("hi").Render(ctx, &buf)` and assert on the HTML. No app needed.
- Handlers: copy `internal/home/handler_test.go`, which runs the app through its
  middleware on a free port. On a bare `a.Mux()`, `web.Render`, `web.PageFrom` and
  `web.AddFlash` panic.
- `web.Enable()` without `web.WithSessions()` has no CSRF check, so a test can post
  a form directly. Use a client whose `CheckRedirect` returns
  `http.ErrUseLastResponse` to see the 303.
